+how pretraining on catalog\_v4 compares with training on pp collisions, read on data generated like pp collisions (the
+pp study's ``from that we decide if we switch''). Design, at no new compute: on the $13$\,TeV pp pools of the $1396$
+hadronic tree classes, the two catalog\_v4 runs of par.~\ref{par:v4_vs_v3} (Muon, two seeds) against the pp-trained
+arm (par.\ The pp-trained arm, \code{FA-ccin2p3-e786bd74}, AdamW, one seed), and each against its own optimizer's
+catalog\_v3 window-trained arm: Muon (the two catalog\_v3 runs of par.~\ref{par:v4_vs_v3}) and AdamW (the dimensionless
+arm the pp-trained one differs from only in sampling, one seed). All four are the dimensionless arm, $4264$ steps, best
+checkpoint at step $4250$, read on the same $2000$ validation events per pool, the MSE of $\log|\mathcal M|^2$; the
+classes grouped by their role in both catalogs as above (for the pp-trained arm a training class is trained on its pp
+events), the $b$ classes apart, each pool split into its events inside catalog\_v3's coverage and the rest
+(\code{analysis/catalog\_v4/pp\_trained\_compare.py}, Fig.~\ref{fig:v4_vs_pp}). Checked first, per probe: the share
+inside the coverage in the AdamW arms' reads equals the share in the mask the Muon reads are split with, on all $1396$
+probes (largest difference $0$: one definition, the same events), and the target's variance in every Muon read
+equals the AdamW reads' to $3\times10^{-4}$ relative. The AdamW arms were trained before the coupling-power fix of the
+two catalog\_v3 BSM pools above.
